<BaseLayout title="BL13 (Feishu) — Lung Shu Point" description="Everything you need to self-treat BL13 (Feishu) — exact location, when to use it, a step-by-step routine, symptom-based point combinations, and expected results." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points/" }, { label: "BL13 (Feishu)", href: "/points/bl13-feishu-acupressure-point/" }]} datePublished="2026-08-19" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">BL13 (Feishu) — Lung Shu</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">BL13, known as Feishu or the Lung Shu point, sits on the upper back beside the third thoracic vertebra. It is the classic back point for coughs, asthma, colds, nasal congestion, and supporting the lungs through cold season.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Bladder (BL)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Upper back, about two finger-widths beside the third thoracic vertebra (T3)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Cough, asthma and wheezing, colds and nasal congestion, weak lungs and immunity</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Easy with a partner or a thumb hooked around from the side</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–2 minutes per side</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–2 times daily</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/cv22-tiantu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">CV22 (Tiantu)</a>, <a href="/points/lu7-lieque-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">LU7 (Lieque)</a>, <a href="/points/gv14-dazhui-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">GV14 (Dazhui)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Recent back or spine injury, or acute, severe chest pain — see a doctor instead (see Precautions)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why BL13 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">BL13 is a Shu (Back-Shu) point on the Bladder meridian, and Back-Shu points are where the energy of each organ gathers on the back. The Lung Shu is the back point for the lungs, and in traditional practice it is used both to assess and to support respiratory health — a tender spot here is often read as a sign that the lungs or respiratory system are under strain. Because it sits beside the upper spine, it is also a natural place for tension to collect when a cough has been going on for a while, with the muscles between the shoulder blades tightening as you repeatedly cough or breathe shallowly.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Shu points are traditionally regarded as nourishing — they are used to strengthen, or tonify, the organ they correspond to, rather than only to relieve acute symptoms. That makes BL13 the point to return to again and again during cold season, for a lingering cough, or whenever you feel run down and prone to chest infections. It is a favorite point for wintertime self-care: press it after a chill, before travel, or at the first tickle in the throat, and pair it with GV14 for the first signs of a cold. Because it sits on the back, it is one of the easiest points to combine with a partner or a smooth massage tool.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/bl13-feishu-location.webp" alt="BL13 acupressure point location on the upper back" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="565" height="300" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">BL13 is on the upper back, roughly one and a half finger-widths (cun) out from the lower edge of the spinous process of the third thoracic vertebra (T3) — about two finger-widths to each side of the spine. A good landmark: the third thoracic vertebra sits about level with the inner end of the spine of the shoulder blade (scapula). To find the point, tilt your head forward to feel the most prominent bump at the base of the neck (C7), then count down two more vertebral bumps to reach T3 — the tender spot a couple of finger-widths to either side of that bump is BL13.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) you are in the muscle band beside the spine, not on the spinous process itself; (2) the level matches the inner edge of the shoulder blade; (3) pressing toward the spine produces a deep, dull, achy sensation rather than a sharp pinch on bone.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use BL13 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-forest-200 bg-forest-50 dark:border-forest-900/40 dark:bg-forest-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You have a cough, especially one that lingers after a cold</li>
          <li>• Your nose is congested or you feel a cold coming on</li>
          <li>• You feel run down, prone to chest infections, or low on respiratory reserve</li>
          <li>• You want a daily maintenance point through cold and flu season</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You recently injured the back, spine, or ribs, or had back surgery</li>
          <li>• You have acute, severe chest pain or sudden difficulty breathing — get medical help</li>
          <li>• There is broken skin, a rash, or tenderness over the upper back</li>
          <li>• A fever is high and not settling, or you feel seriously unwell</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: BL13 is a comfort practice for coughs and congestion, not first aid for chest pain or breathing emergencies.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Cough:</strong> BL13 is a primary point for soothing coughs, especially ones that linger.</li>
      <li><strong>Asthma and wheezing:</strong> It may help support easier breathing between episodes.</li>
      <li><strong>Colds and flu:</strong> It is traditionally used at the start of a cold to support recovery.</li>
      <li><strong>Nasal congestion:</strong> It helps relieve stuffiness and a blocked or runny nose.</li>
      <li><strong>Weak lungs:</strong> As a Back-Shu point it is used to nourish and strengthen the respiratory system.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For a complete step-by-step routine, see our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-back-pain/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">back pain acupressure guide</a>.</p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Massage BL13</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Because the point is on the back, it is easiest to work on while lying face down with help from a partner, or to reach it yourself with a thumb hooked around from the side. Start light and deepen gradually, working toward the spine.</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Lie face down, or sit and have a partner locate the bumps of the upper spine.</li>
      <li>Place a thumb or the heel of the hand about two finger-widths beside the third thoracic vertebra.</li>
      <li>Apply pressure that starts light and deepens slowly, feeling for the muscles beside the spine.</li>
      <li>Press and knead gently in the direction of the spine for 1–2 minutes on each side.</li>
      <li>Repeat daily during cold season or whenever the chest feels congested.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing directly on the bony spinous processes themselves. The target is the muscle band beside the spine — if you feel bone grinding under your thumb, slide out a finger-width or two and stay on the soft tissue.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine BL13 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A full respiratory routine is more effective than pressing one point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 30–60 seconds each.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">First sign of a cold</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL13 → <a href="/points/gv14-dazhui-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">GV14</a> → <a href="/points/lu7-lieque-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">LU7</a>, 30–60s each, up to 2–3 times on the first day</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Lingering cough or chest congestion</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL13 → <a href="/points/cv22-tiantu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">CV22</a> → <a href="/points/cv17-shanzhong-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">CV17</a>, keeping CV22 gentle and brief</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Nasal congestion and stuffiness</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL13 → <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">LI4</a> → LU7, holding each 60s for up to 3 minutes total</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Weak lungs / cold-season maintenance</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL13 → GV14 → CV17 daily; add <a href="/points/cv6-qihai-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">CV6</a> when energy is low</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a fuller program, see the <a href="/body/back-acupressure-points/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">back acupressure points</a> body guide and our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">does acupressure work?</a> research review.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">The point is often noticeably tender, especially during a cold. Most people feel a looser, more open sensation across the upper back and chest within a few minutes, though congestion can return as the day goes on. Do not expect a cure after one press.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With 1–2 sessions per day, tenderness at the point usually eases and breathing often feels fuller and less effortful. This matches the pattern in studies of acupressure for respiratory symptoms, where benefits appear after several days of regular use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For chronic conditions such as asthma or easily-tired lungs, keep BL13 as a complement to your medical care — it supports respiratory comfort but never replaces prescribed treatment. A cough or chest complaint that has not improved after 2–3 weeks, or any acute chest pain, is a signal to see a clinician.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Evidence on acupressure for respiratory and musculoskeletal conditions has grown in recent years. Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure as an adjunct for pain and breathing-related complaints, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. Back points such as the Lung Shu are common in studies of breathing and immune support, and several trials report that pressure at these points may ease cough, congestion, and chest tightness, though the studies are small and methodologically varied.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Researchers consistently note that acupressure trials are hard to blind, because the treatment is physical and participants can usually tell whether they are being treated. This makes it difficult to separate the specific effects of a particular point from the general benefits of touch, pressure, and relaxation. The overall picture — which we discuss in detail on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work/">does acupressure work?</a> review — is one of encouraging signals alongside a clear need for larger, higher-quality studies before firm conclusions can be drawn. For respiratory care, acupressure is best understood as a low-risk comfort measure that supports, but never replaces, medical treatment.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["song-2015"]} note="The review above assessed self-acupressure across respiratory complaints. It is not a study of BL13 in isolation, but BL13 is the point most commonly used for lung and breathing support." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Avoid BL13 if you have a recent spine, rib, or shoulder-blade injury, or recent back surgery.</li>
      <li>Do not press on the bony spinous processes themselves — work on the muscle beside the spine.</li>
      <li>Skip the area if there is skin irritation, a rash, or broken skin over the upper back.</li>
      <li>Do not use this point for acute, severe chest pain — that needs immediate medical care.</li>
      <li>Stop if you feel sharp pain, numbness, or tingling; seek medical care for difficulty breathing or a fever that is not settling.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>Sudden, severe, or crushing chest pain or pressure — seek emergency care</li>
        <li>Shortness of breath that gets worse, or difficulty catching your breath</li>
        <li>Coughing up blood, or a cough that lasts more than 3 weeks without improvement</li>
        <li>High fever, wheezing that does not settle, or signs of a serious respiratory infection</li>
        <li>Back or spine pain that appeared suddenly after a fall, twist, or heavy impact</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Where is BL13 and how do I find it?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">BL13 is on the upper back, about one and a half finger-widths out from the third thoracic vertebra (T3). Find the most prominent bump at the base of your neck (C7), count down two more bumps to reach T3, and press the tender spot two finger-widths to either side.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I press BL13 on myself?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes, by hooking a thumb around from your side and working the muscle beside the spine, though it is easier and more comfortable to have a partner press it while you lie face down. A firm but gentle knead toward the spine for 1–2 minutes per side works well.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I press BL13?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Press and knead gently toward the spine for 1–2 minutes on each side, once or twice daily. During a cold or at the first tickle of the throat, you can repeat the routine 2–3 times in the first day, then settle into a daily maintenance habit.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is BL13 helpful for asthma?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">BL13 is traditionally used to support easier breathing and is a common point in respiratory care, but it is not a substitute for prescribed asthma medication. Use your treatment as directed by your doctor, and treat acupressure as an additional comfort measure between episodes.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor instead of pressing BL13?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">See a doctor for sudden or severe chest pain, worsening shortness of breath, coughing up blood, or a cough that lasts more than 3 weeks. Acute chest pain is never a job for acupressure — get medical help first and treat acupressure as a comfort measure for milder symptoms.</div>
    </details>
    </section>

